    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "Do you ever hear voices or sounds that other people around you cannot hear?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S020",
      "patient_response": "Well, yes, actually. Yes, I do. And it's funny you ask that because it's been happening a lot more lately, especially when I'm working the late shift. It's usually when I'm down in the basement, near the big HVAC units, or sometimes when I'm just putting my supplies away in the mop closet on the third floor. \n\nI'll be standing there, maybe rinsing out the bucket, you know, getting the dirty water out so it doesn't smell the next day, and I'll hear this man's voice. It's not a whisper, Dr. Smith. It's clear as day, like he's standing right there, maybe just over my left shoulder. And he'll say my name. Just my name, real sharp-like. Or sometimes, and this is the part that really gets to me, he'll start telling me that I ruined everything. \"You ruined it all,\" he says. Just like that. \n\nAnd I'll spin around, dropping the mop handle—which makes a terrible clatter on that concrete floor, let me tell you—and there's nobody there. Just the cinderblock walls and the smell of pine cleaner. But the voice, it sounds so real. I even went out into the hallway once, checking all the doors, thinking maybe somebody got locked in after hours, maybe one of the accountants stayed late and was messing with me. But the doors were all locked tight. Nobody was there. Just me and that voice coming out of nowhere, telling me I messed everything up. It's really hard to concentrate on getting the streaks out of the glass when you've got someone invisible yelling at you like that, you know?",
